---
# IS THE SITE ANSWERING, OR ONLY RESPONDING.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE WAS NOTHING HERE UNTIL NOW
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every rule in this directory watches a MACHINE or a PROCESS -- the host's memory, the JVM's heap,
# k3s's replica count, Flux's reconciliation. All of them can be green while the web tier serves a
# 500 to every visitor, because none of them reads a response code. `TargetDown` is the closest and
# it only says the pod answers `/metrics`.
#
# `kinowo_web_http_requests_total` (services/metrics/WebHttpMetrics.scala) is the one series that
# says the site is answering at all, and until this file nothing alerted on it.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY 5xx AND NOT "THE ERROR RATE"
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The obvious rule -- 4xx+5xx as a share of all requests, which is what the "Error share" panel on
# the application-health dashboard draws -- CANNOT BE ALERTED ON HERE, and the reason is worth
# writing down because the panel makes it look like it can.
#
# `status` is the CLASS, not the code (WebHttpMetrics explains why, and why widening it is the wrong
# fix), so the 4xx line cannot separate "a route broke" from the several endpoints whose correct
# answer to an anonymous caller IS a 4xx:
#
#   /api/me, /uptime/*, /tasks/*   admin-gated (controllers.AdminAction) -- 401 to everyone not
#                                  signed in, permanently, by design.
#   /:city/                        404 for any city slug that does not exist, which is what a
#                                  slug-scanning crawler generates all night.
#
# Measured over 2026-08-30..09-04 with the admin surface and the unmatched-route bucket both
# excluded, the 4xx share of PL's matched-route traffic still reaches 92% and sits at a p99 of ~0.3
# -- entirely `/:city/` 404s against a small overnight denominator. There is no threshold that both
# catches a regression and stays quiet through that.
#
# THE EVENT THAT PROMPTED THIS FILE IS THE PROOF. On 2026-09-04 the panel's error share went from
# ~0.5% to ~25% and held there for hours. All of it was `POST /<cc>/uptime/img-event` -- the poster
# telemetry beacon in assets/js/shared.js, which every browser posts and `adminAction` 401s -- rising
# because a JS-executing crawler started walking the US/UK/DE film pages (`/us/:city/movie/:slug`
# went 10x and `/us/:city/movie/og-image` went from nothing to 0.4/s in the same ten minutes). Not
# one 5xx was served. A share alert would have paged for a working site.
#
# So this file alerts on the two things that are unambiguous, and on its own absence.
#
# ⚠️ HISTORY STARTS AT THE 2026-08-29 CUTOVER, and the web tier's own request metric only since the
# Fly proxy metrics were lost. Do not tune these against anything earlier -- there is nothing there.
groups:
  - name: web-errors
    rules:
      - alert: WebServerErrorsServed
        # A SHARE **AND** AN ABSOLUTE FLOOR, because either alone is wrong. The share alone fires
        # when a tier serving six requests a minute happens to throw one; the absolute rate alone
        # cannot tell a 1%-of-traffic blip from every request failing.
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web",status="5xx"}[10m]))
              / on (country)
            sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web"}[10m]))
          ) > 0.02
          and on (country)
          sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web",status="5xx"}[10m])) > 0.01
        # TEN MINUTES. The web tier serves a short 5xx window on every rolling deploy (CLAUDE.md
        # calls that acceptable), and a pod that boots into a crash loop is still red well past
        # this. Shorter and every deploy pages.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} web is serving {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} 5xx"
          description: >-
            The web tier is the only part users see, so this is a user-visible outage rather than a
            degradation. Check the pod first -- `kubectl -n kinowo logs deploy/web-{{ $labels.country }}`
            -- and then whether it is failing to read: a 5xx on the read path is usually Mongo
            (mongodb.rules) or a heap that no longer leaves room to collect (jvm-heap.rules), and
            both have their own rules that should be read alongside this one.

      # PER ROUTE, BECAUSE THE TIER-WIDE SHARE ABOVE CANNOT SEE THE SMALLEST SURFACE THAT MATTERS.
      # Concurrent writes to a signed-in visitor's state lost updates or answered 503 (2026-09; now
      # one atomic write). `WebServerErrorsServed` would never have fired: /api/me/* is under 0.01%
      # of the tier's traffic, so every write could fail without moving a tier-wide 2%. This asks
      # the question per `route`: at least THREE 5xx in 30 minutes (so one unlucky request on a
      # quiet route is not a pattern) and over 10% of that route's responses. 4xx never counts --
      # GET /api/me answers 401 to every anonymous visitor, by design. `.*/api/me` because the
      # Showtimes countries are mounted and `route` carries the mount (`/uk/api/me/...`).
      #
      # A warning, not a page: it is a few people's hidden films, not the site. `for: 10m` on top
      # of the 30-minute window so a rolling deploy's brief 5xx does not qualify.
      - alert: UserStateWritesFailing
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country, route) (increase(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web",route=~".*/api/me.*",status="5xx"}[30m]))
              / on (country, route)
            sum by (country, route) (increase(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web",route=~".*/api/me.*"}[30m]))
          ) > 0.1
          and on (country, route)
          sum by (country, route) (increase(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web",route=~".*/api/me.*",status="5xx"}[30m])) >= 3
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} {{ $labels.route }} is answering 5xx to {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of requests"
          description: >-
            Signed-in visitors' writes to their own state are failing -- a hide that does not
            stick, a language pick that reverts -- while the rest of the tier is fine, which is
            why `WebServerErrorsServed` (a tier-wide share) cannot see it. A 503 here is the
            store refusing the write: `sum by (endpoint, outcome)
            (increase(kinowo_web_user_state_writes_total[1h]))` says whether it is
            `store_failure` (Mongo threw -- the web log has `UserStateRepository.… failed`) or
            `unavailable` (the pod has no users store at all).

      # A MISSING INDEX IS SILENT OTHERWISE. Every web pod builds the unique `userId` index on
      # `userStates` at boot and keeps serving if that fails (failing the boot would take the read
      # tier down over a few signed-in rows) -- so this gauge, and an ERROR in the web log, are the
      # only trace. Duplicate rows for one userId are the usual cause (2026-09-20 found four).
      - alert: UserStateUniqueIndexMissing
        expr: min by (country) (kinowo_web_user_state_userid_index_unique{job="kinowo-web"}) == 0
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} userStates has no unique userId index"
          description: >-
            A web pod could not build the unique `userId` index on `userStates` (the web log has the
            ERROR `userStates has NO unique userId index` with the reason -- usually duplicate rows
            for one userId). Writes keyed on userId may land on either duplicate, and reads may
            disagree with them. Dedupe the rows, then restart a web pod to rebuild the index.

      - alert: WebAssetsNotFound
        # THE ONE 4xx WORTH PAGING ON. `/assets/:file` serves the fingerprinted CSS and JS, so a
        # 404 here is not a crawler -- it is a page that rendered without its stylesheet or its
        # script for every visitor who loaded it. Over 2026-08-30..09-04 this rate never exceeded
        # 0.0006/s across all five countries, so the threshold is ~15x the observed ceiling and
        # still trips within minutes of a broken deploy.
        expr: >-
          sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_web_http_requests_total{job="kinowo-web",route=~".*/assets/:file",status="4xx"}[10m])) > 0.01
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} web is 404ing its own assets"
          description: >-
            Visitors are being served HTML that references an asset the tier will not serve, which
            renders as an unstyled or inert page rather than as an error. The usual cause is a
            fingerprint the deploy changed on one side only -- compare the asset URLs in the
            rendered HTML against what the pod has.

      - alert: WebHttpMetricsAbsent
        # The two rules above are comparisons against a series, so they cannot fire when it stops
        # being scraped -- they go quiet, which reads exactly like a healthy site. This is the
        # tripwire for that, the same shape as the *MetricsAbsent rules in the other files here.
        expr: absent(kinowo_web_http_requests_total)
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "The web tier's request metric has stopped arriving"
          description: >-
            `WebServerErrorsServed` and `WebAssetsNotFound` are both silent while this is firing,
            and their silence means nothing. Either every web pod is gone (k3s.rules,
            monitoring-self.rules) or `WebHttpMetrics` stopped registering -- check `/metrics` on a
            web pod for the `kinowo_web_http_requests_total` family before assuming the scrape.
